/*
time complexity - O(logn)
space complexity - O(1)
INTUITION
Key observations:

A rotated sorted array is made of two sorted segments.

The minimum element is the pivot—the point where rotation happened.

If the current window [l…r] is already sorted (nums[l] < nums[r]):
→ the leftmost element nums[l] is the minimum in this window, so answer is found.

Otherwise:

Check which half is sorted.

If left half (l…mid) is sorted → minimum must be in right half.

If right half (mid…r) is sorted → minimum must be in left half.

You update the answer with nums[mid] every loop.

*/
class Solution {
    public int findMin(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int l =0, r= n-1;
        int ans = nums[0];
        while(l<=r){
            if(nums[l]<nums[r]){
                ans = Math.min(ans,nums[l]);
                break;
            }
            int mid = (l+r)/2;
            ans = Math.min(ans,nums[mid]);
            //l,mid are in sorted segement
            if(nums[l]<=nums[mid])//then search in other segment for min
            {
                l=mid+1;
            }
            //mid,r in sorted segment
            else if(nums[mid]<=nums[r])//search in other segemnt
            {
                r=mid -1;
            }
        }   
        return ans;
    }
}
